ukraine crisis the power of language in  the interview the novelist andre kurkov  we are at the international literature  festival in berlin where you are one of  the participants andrei korkov you are a  ukrainian writer from kiev you speak or  at least you understand 11 languages  welcome to the interview on dw  interview  i speak six the others are gone because  i didn't use them  i suggest  there are more translations of works by  you than by any other ukrainian writer  your latest work is ukraine diaries  dispatches from kiev  it's about the maidan revolution  but it has not yet appeared in ukrainian  or russian  is it just for exports  it was written instead of another book  that i had started in october it was  going to be a collection of essays for  foreign readers about ukraine the way it  was and the way it is today  about the differences between russian  and ukrainian history and about the  differences between the mentalities and  politics of the two countries  but then after the protests began on  november the 21st i realized i couldn't  write a relaxed book in these tense  times  so i called my publishers in austria and  offered them ukraine diaries instead  entries from november the 21st to april  the 24th the events of each day without  regard to what would happen the  following day  ukraine diaries is something of an  exception in your works  it's about current events of interest to  readers in the west right now  your other works including bestsellers  such as death and the penguin are  intended more for ukrainian readers how  do you explain your popularity in the  west and in germany  it's probably because the stories i tell  are gripping and in some way universal  foreign readers can empathize with my  ukrainian protagonists  perhaps the mix of black humor and  absurdist irony in all my books  also has some appeal  you were born in leningrad in russia and  moved to kiev where you still live you  write in russian so are you a russian  writer or a ukrainian writer  ukraine i'm a ukrainian writer of  russian origin who writes in the  language of his heritage the argument  about what i really am has been going on  for 20 years i fight for the rights of  ukrainian authors who write in russian  who identify with ukraine and ukrainian  literature not all ukrainian  intellectuals think like me so the  argument will continue  that means some people think it's not  politically correct to write in russian  in ukraine  russian is the first and greatest victim  of the political situation and the  conflict with russia  nonetheless i write mainly in russian  just a few essays are in ukrainian and  english  russian is spoken more quietly now  because for the very many russian  speakers in ukraine 12 million ethnic  russians and probably 10 to 12 million  ukrainians their mother tongue has  become the language of the aggressor  that's why they now speak more softly  but all your books have been translated  into ukrainian  all the new ones are published  simultaneously in russian and ukrainian  though my children's books are only in  ukrainian none of my books have been  published in russia since 2008. i don't  exist there and in ukraine readers  prefer children's books in ukrainian  you described russian as the language of  the aggressor so what does that mean for  the future as you said it's the mother  tongue of 12 million  these 12 million feel ukrainian  ukrainian citizens who do not need putin  to protect them because they speak  russian  they don't face any bans and don't have  any problem speaking russian there never  was any such problem  politicians invented the language  problem to divide the electorate instead  of discussing ways to develop the  country one faction said it wanted  russian declared an official language  while the other faction promised to  prevent russian ever becoming an  official language  but one of the first things the new  government tried to do was impose  restrictions on russian  many observers considered misguided  it was not an attempt to restrict the  use of russian  and it wasn't the government it was a  member of yushchenko's party called  svyatoslav kirilenko  during a session of parliament he called  for the repeal of a law that yanukovych  had pushed through in 2012 that gave the  languages of minorities official status  as regional languages  in reality the repeal of the law would  have changed nothing and president  poroshenko never signed the repeal so  it's not on the books  this law never used to play any role  it's now become important  de facto they use russian as the  official language in eastern ukraine  both in official communication in the  courts and in other areas as well  there was very little ukrainian spoken  there  the law simply gave russian an official  status in the east of the country  but how do you explain the fact that the  works of gogol for example a great  ukrainian writer who wrote in russian  are taught as foreign literature in  schools  after the transition of power in 1991 or  rather after the country changed its  name without a transition of power the  government decided on a special gesture  it created jobs for ukrainian  nationalists in the areas of information  culture and education but not the  economy  at the same time russian-speaking  culture in ukraine has never been  investigated for example is bulgarkov a  ukrainian or a russian writer  it's a question of  gogol's  identity  as a writer was russian though he  enriched literally high russian with  thousands of ukrainian words  you could say he introduced ukrainian  into russian but i think the  relationship to russian-speaking writers  in ukraine will change  it will become more civilized  i think we'll develop a more inclusive  and less exclusive culture  there's still a powerful group of  intellectuals who think that ukrainian  culture means ethnic ukrainian culture  but there's an even more powerful group  that believes we are a multi-national  state and whatever is created here  whether by hungarians greeks bulgarians  ethnic russians or jews it's all part of  ukrainian culture  this contest this argument will continue  but in the end the more civilized  inclusive position will prevail we  professionally  what role does literature play for you  in these unsettled times  and what role does analysis the  observation of trends in society  since november i've hardly touched  literature i've been busy with essays  and my diary  i've written almost 100 articles about  the situation in donbass crimea and kiev  i write about politics and am more of a  social activist than a writer at the  moment  so as an engineer of the human soul  or as an engineer of a civil society  how do you evaluate current trends in  ukrainian society  are we seeing the birth of a nation  the transformation of a nation  or  we're seeing the recovery after a  collapse caused by politicians  ten years ago it was correct to say that  one part of ukraine was pro-russian and  another pro-ukrainian  but now thanks to yanukovych and putin  the country has come together  all the regions apart from donbass and  crimea have become pro-european  they've all come to appreciate the  importance of a united country  at the same time we're also seeing a  transformation a tolerant and peaceful  society has been radicalized i think  we'll see a permanent state of war and  conflict in the east  i don't wish to repeat russian  propaganda about what it calls a fascist  hunter  but what about the disturbing tendencies  in western ukraine such as the  nationalist rights sector party  if a hunter were in power in kiev it  would have simply bombed donbass to  smithereens generals would be in power  tanks  ukrainian politicians have always been  and still  ukraine is moving from an ambivalent  political balancing act between east and  west  to a more decisive form of government  and that will probably lead to a  crackdown on corruption at least i very  much hope so  at the same time i think it's very  important for ukrainians to remain  tolerant towards people with different  backgrounds  i've been arguing with the nationalists  for more than two decades we respect  each other we accept our differences and  we don't hurl abuse at each other i hope  all other ukrainians who disagree about  things will use their tongues and not  their muscles  on tuesday an association agreement  between ukraine and the european union  was ratified  but a central element a full customs  union will be implemented later what do  you think about that  is  i was a bit surprised that it's a  victory seized from europe and not  ukraine this probably weakens poroshenko  on the other hand it all depends on what  further steps ukraine and europe take  some european parliamentarians are  saying that the decision to postpone the  customs union is not final and could be  soon changed or amended  thank you
 

